# Runtime state: the frame cache, the hook log, and the pid/claim files that
# track which pane holds which Pokemon. All of it is rebuilt on demand and the
# cache alone is tens of megabytes.
#
#   npm run warm     rebuilds the frame cache
.state/

# Downloaded sprites.
#
# The guests are a cache — over twelve hundred Pokemon that arrive when named
# and are evicted when the disk is wanted — so they stay out.
#
# The residents are not. They are the shipped roster: pinned, hand-chosen, and
# the reason a fresh clone works without downloading anything. They are also the
# only way the gallery in the README can animate, since a PNG cannot.
#
# Adding a resident that downloads its sprites means adding a line here.
# `npm test` fails if this list and the roster disagree.
assets/pokemon/*
!assets/pokemon/bulbasaur/
!assets/pokemon/charmander/
!assets/pokemon/eevee/
!assets/pokemon/haunter/
!assets/pokemon/jigglypuff/
!assets/pokemon/meowth/
!assets/pokemon/munchlax/
!assets/pokemon/pidgey/
!assets/pokemon/psyduck/
!assets/pokemon/squirtle/

# Built from config.json by `npm run build`.
build/

# Notes to ourselves about what to build next. Kept on disk, out of the repo.
docs/roadmap.md

# The hand-picked sprites in assets/ are NOT ignored, and must not be. They were
# found by hand, cannot be re-downloaded, and several are the whole reason a
# given entry looks right — Pikachu's pair, Ash, Charizard firing, Psyduck,
# Meowth jumping, Gengar moving, Cubone, the Pokeballs. Losing them would mean
# losing work no command can redo.

.DS_Store
node_modules/
*.log
